Ananya Venkataraman plays Saraswati veena through a dual path: one completely acoustic microphone chain and one contact-mic chain used only for transient control and low resonance. She avoids continuous effects during lead passages, preserving finger noise, string buzz, and the physical resistance of gamaka bends.

Marcus Vale operates drum machines, analog sequencers, and mono-synth bass. His role is intentionally restrained: the kick grid stays stable enough to make the acoustic rhythmic displacement legible, while bass sequences change only when the ensemble's longer cycles resolve.

Karthik Raman plays muted mridangam, kanjira, ceramic percussion, and metal objects. He acts as the bridge between tala structure and machine pulse, often introducing a phrase acoustically before Marcus mirrors only part of it electronically.

